We demonstrate that the decay width ratios for the semileptonic processes $\Lambda_b 	o \Lambda c \ell  ar{
u}$ and $\Lambda_b 	o \Lambda c^* \ell  ar{
u}$ diverge from the heavy quark limit with a magnitude of approximately 10%. This deviation is notably larger than the corresponding ratios observed in $B 	o D \ell  ar{
u}$ and $B 	o D^* \ell  ar{
u}$ decays. Such findings suggest that $\Lambda_b 	o \Lambda c (	ext{vector/axial})$ decays could serve as an effective tool for determining the CKM matrix element $V_{cs}$.

The Large Hadron Collider's (LHCb) collaboration has recently reported significant discrepancies in the $F_1$ and $F_2$ semileptonic form factors for $B 	o D^* \ell  ar{
u}_\ell$ transitions, with values deviating from Standard Model predictions by approximately 3.0$\sigma$ and $2.0\sigma$, respectively. This paper explores the implications of these potential anomalies for the $B 	o \pi \ell  ar{
u}_\ell$ channel, utilizing the Standard Model EFT framework. We examine a scenario where a new physics contribution, characterized by a non-zero Wilson coefficient $C_{V_L}$, accounts for these anomalies. Our analysis reveals that this same $C_{V_L}$ operator can explain the observed tension in the $B 	o \pi \ell  ar{
u}_\ell$ sector, with a predicted shift in the branching ratio of roughly 10\%.
